{
  "comprehensible": "The house was in a condition of dignified decline, because the people who lived there had moved away to mainland Europe a long time ago.",
  "glosses": [
    {
      "expression": "genteel decay",
      "gloss": "a state of decline that still keeps a certain refined, upper-class quality"
    },
    {
      "expression": "repaired to",
      "gloss": "went to; moved to (a formal or slightly old-fashioned use)"
    },
    {
      "expression": "the Continent",
      "gloss": "mainland Europe (often used by British speakers, especially historically)"
    }
  ],
  "grammar": "The phrase 'its occupants having long since repaired to the Continent' is an absolute construction: it has its own subject ('its occupants') and a participle ('having repaired'), and it explains why the house is in that state. It is not a separate main clause, so it may at first look like a fragment.",
  "translation": "这栋房子处于一种体面而衰败的状态，因为它的住户早已迁往欧洲大陆。"
}

